      "side": 1,
      "message": "We still need SDFMaskFilterImpl::filterMask (which calls SkComputeDistanceFieldSize upfront) to reject dimensions that fail DFScratch validation before calling SkMaskBuilder::PrepareDestination:\n\n1. For dimensions like 4071x65527 (from the b/576664769 reproducer), the output SDF size (4079 * 65535 \u003d 267 MB) fits in INT_MAX, so PrepareDestination succeeds while DFScratch (4.81 GB) overflows int.\n2. In bounds-only mode (src.fImage \u003d\u003d nullptr, called by SkScalerContext::internalMakeGlyph at SkScalerContext.cpp:303), SDFMaskFilterImpl::filterMask returns before calling SkGenerateDistanceFieldFrom* / DFScratch. If the bounds-only pass returns true (setting origGlyph.fMaskFormat \u003d kSDF_Format) and the subsequent image pass in SkScalerContext::getImage returns false in DFScratch, SkScalerContext::getImage hits SkASSERT_RELEASE(srcMask.fFormat \u003d\u003d origGlyph.fMaskFormat) at SkScalerContext.cpp:786 (and PrepareDestination allocates 267 MB with SK_MALLOC_THROW before DFScratch rejects it).\n3. Checking the valid side of the INT_MAX boundary via SkComputeDistanceFieldSize also avoids allocating 2 GB in the unit test (which fails sk_calloc_canfail on 32-bit x86 bots and runs the 4-pass distance transform over ~119M pixels).\n\nTo avoid duplicating the math or leaking DFData details into the header, I moved SkComputeDistanceFieldSize to SkDistanceFieldGen.cpp and had it share DFScratch\u0027s size check.",
